# Setting up real accounts and shared data

This app used to store everything in each person's own browser, with a
login screen that accepted any email/password. It's now wired up to run
on Supabase (real accounts, one shared database, row-level security so
loan officers only see their own deals). Here's how to turn it on.

## 1. Create your Supabase project

I can't do this step for you — it requires creating an account. Go to
https://supabase.com, sign up (free tier is enough to start), and create
a new project. Pick a strong database password and save it somewhere
safe (you likely won't need it day-to-day, Supabase manages the
connection for you).

## 2. Run the schema

In your Supabase project: **SQL Editor > New query**, paste the entire
contents of `supabase/schema.sql` from this repo, and click **Run**.
This creates all the tables, security rules, and the trigger that
auto-creates a profile whenever someone signs in for the first time.

## 3. Get your API keys

**Project Settings > API**. You need two values:
- `Project URL`
- `anon public` key (NOT the `service_role` key — that one must never
  end up in this app's code or in your browser)

## 4. Configure the app

Copy `.env.example` to `.env` in the project root and fill in the two
values from step 3:

```
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-public-key
```

`.env` is already in `.gitignore` — it won't get committed.

## 5. Create yourself as the first admin

1. Run the app (`npm install`, then `npm run dev`) and go to the login
   screen.
2. In the Supabase dashboard: **Authentication > Users > Add user** —
   create an account with your own email and a password you choose.
3. Back in the app, sign in with that email/password.
4. In Supabase: **SQL Editor**, run:
   ```sql
   update public.profiles set role = 'admin' where email = 'you@eastcoastmortgage.com';
   ```
   (use your real email). Sign out and back in — you're now an admin.

## 6. Deploy the invite function (so you can add loan officers from the app)

The "Add Loan Officer" / "Add Admin" buttons in User Management call a
small server-side function that creates the person's login and emails
them an invite. It needs to be deployed once via the Supabase CLI:

```bash
npm install -g supabase
supabase login
supabase link --project-ref your-project-ref
supabase functions deploy invite-user
```

Until this is deployed, those two buttons will show an error — everything
else in the app works without it.

## 7. Invite your loan officers

Once step 6 is done, use **User Management > Add Loan Officer** in the
app itself. They'll get an email invite to set their own password, and
their profile (NMLS #, goals, license states, etc.) will already be
filled in from what you entered.

## What changed, in plain terms

- Login is now real: only people you've invited can sign in, with their
  own password.
- Each loan officer only sees their own deals, goals, and compliance
  records. You (admin) see everything. This is enforced by the database
  itself, not just hidden in the UI — so it holds even if someone pokes
  at the app directly.
- Data is shared: everyone is looking at the same database now, not
  their own private browser storage.
- The old "Demo credentials: any email/password" login and the
  self-selected "I am an Admin" toggle are gone.

## Later: mobile app

You mentioned wanting a mobile app once the web version is solid. Once
this is stable, the same Supabase backend can be reused by a React
Native app (or a simple wrapped/PWA version) without changing the
database or security rules at all — that part of the work is already
done. Happy to help with that whenever you're ready.

